t repeat the origin check for destination 8 or 13; it rests on the recorded delivery. At run time, Deliveries (packages/scope/src/delivery.ts:139 and 195-203) checks the application origin of every publish-file tell on destinations 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 13 before anything is recorded, with the pin's own applicationPublicationOrigin for 7, 8 and 13 and the generic one otherwise; a failed check returns UNVERIFIED and records nothing. Offline replay (packages/replay/src/verify.ts:1792-1797) calls #applicationOrigin for a publish-file delivery only when the destination pin is 4, 5, 6 or 7, and the outcome path (:1869-1872) applies publicationOrigin for the same four pins only. For 8 and 13 it skips both. What replay still does for such a delivery (:1800-1826): it proves the source entry holds the send at that ordinal, verifies the sender's pinned genesis where the handler requires a pin, and derives the judgment again with judgeDelivery over the recorded source entry. So the only trace of the runtime's origin check in a destination 8 or 13 journal is that the delivery entry exists; replay cannot tell an admitted publish-file from one the runtime should have refused. Should it repeat the check? In my judgment yes, for consistency: replay's own rule (verify.ts:1866-1867) is that what it cannot derive is unsupported-definition, never consistent, and today a consistent verdict on a destination 13 journal, the default for new installs, is weaker than one on destination 7 without saying so. Doing it changes replay verdicts (consistent can become mismatch or incomplete for existing 8 and 13 journals, never the reverse), needs the pin-specific policy that the runtime uses (replay holds one run-wide #applicationPublicationOrigin, verify.ts:429 and 1079-1080, and stops if supplied versions disagree), and so needs its own design note, as the note concludes. Not a builder request from this review.\n\nNonblocking notes:\n- The note is written at c8a3fb24 and says so, but the head sits on 1f3feea2, whose predicate landing already removed 17 of the inline destination lists counted in TD-11 and shrank destination.ts to 209,473 bytes. One sentence in the status line recording that would keep the counts honest after landing; the findings stand either way.\n- The status line says \"review draft\". After landing, set it to the landed form of the design-note status convention.\n- The short-form citations (for example wrangler.grow.jsonc:3 after the full path) are fine to read but defeat a mechanical check; the full path each time costs little.\n\nArchitecture: the note separates deploy hazards from gate cost and plan-order debt, states for every item who uses it, what it costs now and whether recorded bytes change, and routes every change of recorded meaning to a design note. That is the right shape for a review that other agents will file requests from.\n\nSecurity: the two top findings are real deploy hazards and correctly first: a literal deploy from the grow or lending file reopens register mode, and no file holds the creation-pin allowance that the R6 landing now requires, which my R6 landing check also flagged. TD-12 closes a gap in the entry-point witness of finding 2.5 for the Worker that takes all new rooms. Nothing in the note weakens a control; BR-6 (declaration seals) is correctly held for the checker's agreement because the seals belong to the 6dd06d03 inventory.\n\nSimplification: none needed in the note itself. It is long because it carries its evidence; section 3 lets a reader stop early.\n\nFindings: none blocking.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4a3c080524030d9a22f5ca0f56df8bc7740ebe19","binding":"assigned","head":"650f0c535b0b86e8499439776e44590ebcd4d51f","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:224b5b7153e62418db53041de343d35b78dcfba5\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:482d8bafc2aed5192a7ed5d2